  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cold front on Sunday causing increased winds and scattered\n   showers.\n\n-  Weak disturbance on Monday Night into Tuesday bringing more \n   light showers.\n\n-  Ridge of high pressure through the end of the week.\n\nA cold front is still on track to push through the Northern\nRockies on Sunday. This front will cause gusty westerly winds with\nmost valleys experiencing gusts of 20 to 30 mph on Sunday. While \nthe high terrain along the Continental Divide should have gusts \ngreater than 40 mph. These westerly winds will create choppy \nconditions on area lakes, especially Flathead. The shower activity\nassociated with the front appears to be mainly confined to north \ncentral Idaho up into northwest Montana. Snow levels will be \nlowering to around 5000 feet across the region with the best \nchance for precipitation. Rain and snowfall will be fairly light. \n\nA weak disturbance is expected to push through the region Monday\nnight into Tuesday. This feature will have a slightly better\nmoisture tap so the shower activity will be more widespread. Snow\nlevels appear to be between 6000 to 7000 feet during the passage\nof this feature, so snow impacts should be limited even at the\npass levels.  \n\nA significant ridge of high pressure is expected to build over the\nregion Wednesday through the end of the week causing valley\ninversions to develop across the Northern Rockies. These valley\ninversions have the potential to allow fog and low stratus to form\nwhich in turn will limit the potential warming the valley bottoms\nwill experience. The ridge shifts to the east by the weekend\ncausing the upper level flow to become more southwesterly over the\nNorthern Rockies. The increased flow aloft should help slowly \nbreak up the valley inversion.",
